---
import DocsLayout from "~/layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Code from "~/components/Code.astro";
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Functions"
  current="functions"
  description="Functions, lambdas, chaining, and currying in kex."
>
  <h1>Functions</h1>

  <p>
    Functions are the backbone of kex. They can be single expressions or
    <code>do … end</code> blocks, and they compose through function chaining.
  </p>

  <h2>Signatures</h2>
  <Code
    code={`let double(n: Integer) = n * 2

# Haskell-style type declaration, then multiple clauses that
# pattern-match on the argument:
factorial : Integer -> Integer
let factorial(0) = 1
let factorial(n: Integer) = n * factorial(n - 1)`}
    file="fns.kex"
    source="examples/fns.kex"
  />

  <h2>Function chaining</h2>
  <p>
    <code>value.f(arg)</code> is equivalent to <code>f(value, arg)</code>. The
    receiver type decides which overload resolves. This is what makes fluent
    chains possible without methods living on the data.
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`requests
  .filter { |req| req.path.startsWith?("/admin") }
  .reject(&.authenticated?)
  .map { |req| SecurityEvent.from(req) }
  .take(20)

# exactly equivalent:
map(requests, ~normalize)`}
    file="ufcs.kex"
    source="examples/ufcs.kex"
  />

  <h2>Lambdas and block arguments</h2>
  <Code
    code={`let inc = { |x| x + 1 }
let add = { |a, b| a + b }

[1, 2, 3].map(~inc)         # [2, 3, 4]
[1, 2, 3].map(&.to(String)) # method reference via &.`}
    file="lambdas.kex"
    source="examples/lambdas.kex"
  />

  <h2>Capturing, currying and partial application</h2>
  <p>
    <code>~func</code> captures a function as a value — on its own, <code
      >~f</code
    > is just <code>f</code>. Each trailing <code>(args)</code> group partially applies
    it: bound arguments fill left to right, and <code>_</code> marks an explicit open
    slot. Once enough arguments arrive to fully saturate, the function runs immediately.
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`let add(a, b) = a + b
let multiply(a, b) = a * b

[1, 2, 3, 4].filter(~even?)       # plain capture — no argument group

let inc    = ~add(1)              # {|b| add(1, b)}
let double = ~multiply(2)         # {|b| multiply(2, b)}

[1, 2, 3].map(~multiply(10))      # [10, 20, 30]
(1..100).reduce(0, ~(+))          # 5050

let sub5 = ~(-)(_, 5)             # {|a| a - 5}
sub5(20)                          # 15`}
    file="curry.kex"
    source="examples/curry.kex"
  />
  <p>
    Every binary operator can be captured with <code>~(op)</code>, plus <code
      >~(!)</code
    > for negation. <code>~(&amp;&amp;)</code> and <code>~(||)</code> are ordinary
    functions, so they evaluate both arguments rather than short-circuiting. The captured
    name may also be module-qualified, to any depth.
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`flags.map(~(!))                   # {|b| !b}

["a", "b"].each(~IO.printLine)
["x", "y"].map(~String.upperCase)
words.map(~Outer.Inner.Deep.shout)`}
  />
  <p>
    Capturing is always spelled with <code>~</code>. <code>&amp;</code> is receiver
    shorthand only, as in <code>&amp;.method</code> — <code>&amp;func</code> and
    <code>&amp;.+</code> are not valid syntax.
  </p>

  <h2>Type-directed <code>make</code></h2>
  <p>
    Behavior attaches to a type through <code>make</code>. Inside,
    <code>@field</code> is shorthand for <code>this.field</code>, and operators
    overload per receiver type.
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`make Vector2D do
  let +(other: Vector2D) -> Vector2D do
    return Vector2D { x: @x + other.x, y: @y + other.y }
  end

  let to(String) -> String do
    return "(\${@x}, \${@y})"
  end
end`}
    file="make.kex"
    source="examples/make.kex"
  />
</DocsLayout>
